//
// loft#1423's second half, in its own file because a firing `@EXPECT_ERROR` stops the run.
//
// `(N-Index)` types `v[i]` as `τ?`, and for a TUPLE the model has no representation for an
// absent one (`formal/types-history.md` D-Opt-NoNull) — so an UNDISCHARGED member read has
// nothing to answer with.  The refusal is right; what was wrong is that the receiver matched
// none of the tuple branches, the member NUMBER reached the field-name parser, and the report
// was *"Expect a field name"* — a message about a name, for a program that wrote a number, on a
// receiver whose real problem is the `?`.  A nullable STRUCT receiver reads correctly through
// its absence (`(L-Null-Which)`), which is what makes the tuple's answer read as a bug.
//
// The discharges the message names all work and are asserted in
// `1424-a-tuple-default-is-its-members-defaults.loft` — a cure a diagnostic advertises has to be
// guarded, or the message is the next defect.
//
// @falsified-at: d6e665ae — interpret exit 1 -> 0 and expectations FAIL/4 -> ok, native the
//   same.  Each member-read cell reports "Expect a field name" and a formatter cascade there,
//   and the destructure cell reports "Cannot destructure a non-tuple value".

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `.0` on `(integer, integer)?`
fn stack_member(i: integer) -> integer { v: vector<(integer, integer)> = [(1, 2)]; v[i].0 }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `.1` on `(text, integer)?`
fn stored_member(i: integer) -> integer { w: vector<(text, integer)> = [("a", 1)]; w[i].1 }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `.0` on `(float, integer)?`
fn through_a_local(i: integer) -> float {
  u: vector<(float, integer)> = [(1.5, 2)];
  e = u[i];
  e.0
}

// A DESTRUCTURE of the same value said *"Cannot destructure a non-tuple value"* — which is not
// true, and sends the author looking at the wrong half of their program.  Same cure, named.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot destructure `(character, integer)?`
fn destructure_it(i: integer) -> integer {
  c: vector<(character, integer)> = [('x', 2)];
  (a, b) = c[i];
  b
}
